<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Catholic University of Leuven</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1834">1834</a>–<a href="/years/1968">1968</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Catholic University of Louvain</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Catholic University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leuven">Leuven</a> was founded by the Belgian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> in 1834 and settled in Leuven in 1835, taking up the tradition of the medieval University of Leuven of 1425. Disputes between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>- and French-speakers came to a head in 1968, when the university was split into the Dutch-speaking KU Leuven and the French-speaking Université catholique de Louvain, which moved to the new town of Louvain-la-Neuve.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/catholic-university-of-leuven.jpg" alt="Catholic University of Leuven" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Université catholique de Louvain - Katholieke Universiteit te Leuven · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sedes_Sapientiae_Univ._Cathol._Lovaniensis,_U.C.L._1909.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
